                <h1 class="content-title">Problem Management & <span class="gradient-text">KEDB</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Master root-cause analysis (RCA), structured problem task decomposition, Known Error Database (KEDB) publishing, 
                    and automated incident workaround propagation in ServiceNow.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: Mission & ITIL Alignment -->
            <section class="content-section" id="problem-mission">
                <h2>Core Mission & Reactive vs Proactive Problem Management</h2>
                <p>
                    According to ITIL v4, the primary purpose of <strong>Problem Management</strong> is to 
                    <em>"reduce the likelihood and impact of incidents by identifying actual and potential causes of incidents, 
                    and managing workarounds and known errors."</em>
                </p>
                <p>
                    While Incident Management focuses strictly on restoration speed, Problem Management performs the deep engineering 
                    forensics required to permanently eradicate recurring disruptions or architect permanent structural workarounds.
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Reactive Problem Management</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Triggered immediately following high-severity incidents (P1/P2 Major Incidents) or repeated ticket thresholds 
                            on the same Configuration Item (CI). Focuses on diagnosing the immediate root cause, formulating emergency workarounds, 
                            and initiating permanent Change Requests (RFCs).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Proactive Problem Management</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Leverages Performance Analytics, machine learning (Predictive Intelligence), and trend analysis across historical 
                            telemetry and incident logs to detect systemic vulnerabilities, capacity bottlenecks, or software degradation before 
                            outages manifest to end users.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Process Boundary: Incident vs. Problem vs. Change</div>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Incident:</strong> Restores service operation rapidly (e.g., restart service, failover database).<br>
                        <strong>Problem:</strong> Identifies underlying root cause and designs permanent fix (e.g., buffer overflow in library v2.1).<br>
                        <strong>Change:</strong> Safely authorizes and implements the permanent software patch in production.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: Table Architecture & Schema -->
            <section class="content-section" id="table-schema">
                <h2>Table Architecture & Key Fields</h2>
                <p>
                    Problem Management extends the foundational <code>task</code> table, maintaining direct foreign-key relationships 
                    with Incidents, Configuration Items (CIs), Knowledge Articles, and Change Requests:
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Table Name</th>
                            <th>Parent Extension</th>
                            <th>Operational Scope</th>
                            <th>Key Dictionary Columns</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>problem</code></td>
                            <td><code>task</code></td>
                            <td>Master Problem investigation record.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>first_reported_by_task</code> (Ref), <code>workaround</code> (HTML/String), 
                                <code>cause_notes</code> (String), <code>resolution_code</code> (Choice), 
                                <code>known_error</code> (Boolean), <code>confirmed_at</code> (DateTime), <code>duplicate_of</code> (Ref)
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>problem_task</code></td>
                            <td><code>task</code></td>
                            <td>Decomposed diagnostic work packages assigned to technical teams.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>problem</code> (Ref to parent), <code>problem_task_type</code> (Choice: General, Root Cause Analysis, Workaround), 
                                <code>state</code> (Choice), <code>started_at</code> (DateTime)
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>m2m_incident_problem</code></td>
                            <td>None (Base M2M)</td>
                            <td>Many-to-many link between multiple incidents and problems.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>incident</code> (Ref to <code>incident</code>), <code>problem</code> (Ref to <code>problem</code>)
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kb_knowledge</code></td>
                            <td>None (Base Table)</td>
                            <td>Central repository housing Known Error Articles.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>kb_knowledge_base</code>, <code>article_type</code>, <code>workflow_state</code>, <code>source</code>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: State Model & Lifecycle -->
            <section class="content-section" id="state-lifecycle">
                <h2>Problem State Model Lifecycle</h2>
                <p>
                    Out-of-the-box ServiceNow Problem Management utilizes a state model designed to enforce 
                    proper stage gates from initial logging through root cause discovery, workaround documentation, and fix verification:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Problem Management State Flow Lifecycle</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TD
                        NEW["New (101)<br>Problem record logged from Incident or Trend Analysis"] --> ASSESS["Assess (102)<br>Problem Coordinator validates scope, priority & assignment"]
                        ASSESS --> RCA["Root Cause Analysis (103)<br>Technical resolver squads investigate underlying failure"]
                        
                        RCA -->|Workaround Identified| KEDB["Publish Known Error & Workaround<br>(known_error = true)"]
                        RCA --> FIX["Fix in Progress (104)<br>Permanent fix engineered or Change Request (RFC) submitted"]
                        
                        FIX --> RESOLV["Resolved (106)<br>Fix verified & operational health stabilized"]
                        RESOLV --> CLOSED["Closed (107)<br>Permanent solution validated & closed complete"]
                        
                        ASSESS -->|Invalid / Duplicate| CANCEL["Canceled (108)<br>Marked duplicate or not reproducible"]
                        RCA -->|Risk Accepted / No Fix| ACCRISK["Closed (107) - Risk Accepted<br>Resolution code: Closed Risk Accepted"]
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="tabs-container">
                    <div class="tabs-header">
                        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-states">Problem State Integers</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-resolutions">Resolution Codes</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-reopen">Reopen & Re-evaluation Policy</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-states">
                        <h3>Problem Lifecycle State Definitions</h3>
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>State Label</th>
                                    <th>Integer Value</th>
                                    <th>Stage Gate Description & Criteria</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>New</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>101</code></td>
                                    <td>Problem created but unassigned or awaiting assessment by Problem Management coordinator.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Assess</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>102</code></td>
                                    <td>Coordinator validates whether problem investigation is justified and assigns owner group.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Root Cause Analysis</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>103</code></td>
                                    <td>Technical teams execute diagnostic tasks (PTASKs) to determine cause and identify workarounds.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Fix in Progress</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>104</code></td>
                                    <td>Root cause identified. A permanent fix is being coded or a Change Request (RFC) is scheduled.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Resolved</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>106</code></td>
                                    <td>Permanent fix has been deployed or permanent workaround established. Mandatory resolution code required.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Closed</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>107</code></td>
                                    <td>Final read-only state. Solution validated over evaluation period.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Canceled</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>108</code></td>
                                    <td>Record canceled due to duplicate logging or invalid technical assessment.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-resolutions">
                        <h3>Resolution Codes (<code>resolution_code</code>)</h3>
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Resolution Code Label</th>
                                    <th>Integer Choice</th>
                                    <th>Operational Meaning</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>Solved (Permanently)</code></td>
                                    <td><code>101</code></td>
                                    <td>Underlying defect eradicated via software release, firmware patch, or architectural replacement.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>Solved (Workaround)</code></td>
                                    <td><code>102</code></td>
                                    <td>Permanent fix not feasible or too costly; approved workaround documented and active in KEDB.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>Canceled (Duplicate)</code></td>
                                    <td><code>103</code></td>
                                    <td>Problem is a duplicate of an existing active problem record (linked via <code>duplicate_of</code>).</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>Closed Risk Accepted</code></td>
                                    <td><code>104</code></td>
                                    <td>Business and Architecture leadership formally approve accepting residual risk without fixing.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-reopen">
                        <h3>Reopening & Governance Rules</h3>
                        <p>
                            ServiceNow enforces strict governance over closed problem tickets:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><strong>Resolved Tickets:</strong> Can be returned to <em>Root Cause Analysis (103)</em> if post-deployment monitoring indicates the recurrence of symptoms.</li>
                            <li><strong>Closed Tickets:</strong> Once transitioned to <code>Closed (107)</code>, records become strictly read-only. If a recurrence occurs later, a new Problem must be created referencing the previous sys_id.</li>
                            <li><strong>Mandatory Fields:</strong> Moving to <em>Resolved</em> mandates completing <code>cause_notes</code> and <code>resolution_code</code>.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: Problem Tasks (PTASK) -->
            <section class="content-section" id="problem-tasks">
                <h2>Problem Task Decomposition (<code>problem_task</code>)</h2>
                <p>
                    Complex infrastructure and application problems require coordinated investigation across multiple disciplines. 
                    Rather than reassigning the master problem record back and forth, Problem Coordinators spawn parallel 
                    <strong>Problem Tasks (PTASKs)</strong> assigned to specialized teams:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Parallel Problem Task (PTASK) Decomposition</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TD
                        PRB["Master Problem (PRB0010042)<br>VPN Gateway Disconnections During Peak Hours"]
                        
                        PRB --> PTASK1["PTASK0001: Root Cause Analysis<br>Assignment: Network Engineering<br>Action: Analyze firewall drop tables & packet MTU"]
                        PRB --> PTASK2["PTASK0002: Root Cause Analysis<br>Assignment: Cloud Platform Team<br>Action: Check AWS Transit Gateway throughput limits"]
                        PRB --> PTASK3["PTASK0003: Workaround Generation<br>Assignment: Service Desk Level 2<br>Action: Create user script to route through Secondary Tunnel"]
                        
                        PTASK1 --> SYNTH["Problem Coordinator Synthesizes Findings"]
                        PTASK2 --> SYNTH
                        PTASK3 --> SYNTH
                        
                        SYNTH --> RFC["Submit Normal Change Request (CHG)<br>Upgrade Gateway Firmware to v5.1.2"]
                    </div>
                </div>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Problem Task Type</th>
                            <th>Choice Value</th>
                            <th>Description & Assigned Responsibility</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Root Cause Analysis</code></td>
                            <td><code>rca</code></td>
                            <td>Technical investigation to determine the exact failure mechanism, hardware flaw, or bug.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Workaround</code></td>
                            <td><code>workaround</code></td>
                            <td>Tasked specifically with discovering, testing, and documenting a temporary operational workaround.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>General</code></td>
                            <td><code>general</code></td>
                            <td>Follow-up administrative, vendor escalation, or audit tasks related to the problem record.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: Known Error Database (KEDB) -->
            <section class="content-section" id="kedb-management">
                <h2>Known Error Database (KEDB) & Workarounds</h2>
                <p>
                    A <strong>Known Error</strong> is a problem that has a documented root cause and an approved workaround. 
                    Publishing Known Errors to the Knowledge Base (KEDB) empowers Service Desk resolvers to restore user incidents 
                    in minutes without waiting for permanent code deployment.
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Known Error Article Generation</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Clicking <em>"Create Known Error article"</em> on a problem record populates a standardized 
                            KEDB article template directly in <code>kb_knowledge</code>, automatically mapping:
                            <strong>Problem Statement</strong>, <strong>Root Cause Analysis</strong>, and <strong>Step-by-Step Workaround</strong>.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Contextual Search Integration</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            When a Service Desk agent types an issue into an Incident's <em>Short Description</em>, 
                            Contextual Search automatically surfaces matching Known Error articles, enabling one-click attachment 
                            and resolution copying.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Retiring Known Errors Post-Fix</div>
                    <p>
                        When the permanent Change Request is deployed and the Problem moves to <code>Closed Complete (107)</code>, 
                        the associated Known Error article in <code>kb_knowledge</code> is automatically updated or scheduled 
                        for retirement so agents do not apply obsolete workarounds.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 6: Cascade Sync -->
            <section class="content-section" id="cascade-sync">
                <h2>Incident Cascade & Workaround Synchronization</h2>
                <p>
                    When multiple Incidents are linked to a single master Problem (via <code>incident.problem_id</code> or 
                    <code>m2m_incident_problem</code>), ServiceNow provides out-of-the-box business rules that synchronize communications 
                    across all active records:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Communicate Workaround Action</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            When a Problem Coordinator updates the <code>workaround</code> field and triggers <em>"Communicate Workaround"</em>, 
                            a business rule executes <code>IncidentUtils.copyProblemWorkaround()</code> to append the workaround into the 
                            <em>Work Notes</em> of all open linked incidents.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Automated Cascade Resolution</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            When a Problem record transitions to <code>Resolved (106)</code>, the system prompts the coordinator whether to 
                            cascade resolution to all linked incidents. If confirmed, child incidents update to <code>Resolved (6)</code> 
                            with identical close codes and notes.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

